shutdown -- Command

Shut down the COHERENT system
/etc/shutdown halt|single|reboot minutes

/etc/shutdown takes the machine down in an orderly way.  It warns every
logged-in user once a minute for the number of minutes you ask for, warns them
again ten seconds before the end, and then hands over to init, which kills
every process and spawns the single-user shell.  That shell reads /.profile,
which is the other half of the program: it unmounts what /etc/rc mounted and,
for the halt and reboot levels, runs /etc/halt.  Neither half is any use
without the other.

The LEVEL must be one of three words:

halt Stop the machine.  /etc/halt unmounts what it can, syncs every
     filesystem so that none comes back up dirty, and stops the processor.  Use
     this when you intend to switch the power off.

single
     Bring the machine down to single-user mode, with the filesystems other
     than the root unmounted.  Use this to run check(1) or to install a
     kernel.

reboot
     The same as halt, and it says so.  This machine cannot restart itself in
     software (see reboot), so the level is accepted and recorded, /.profile
     reports that there is no warm restart, and the machine halts.  You then
     press reset.

MINUTES is a whole number from 0 to 30.  Zero shuts the system down at once.

Only root can run shutdown, and it must be run from the root directory: a
shell sitting on /usr holds that filesystem busy, and the unmount in /.profile
would then fail on exactly the filesystem the shutdown exists to detach.  It
refuses with a reason in both cases rather than half way through.

You do not need to type sync afterwards.  The sync is what clears the dirty
flag in each super block, so it is the last thing the shutdown does.

Notes
This page REPLACES the COHERENT 4.2 Lexicon article of the same name, because
what this system ships is a port of the 4.2 script with a different set of
levels and no third-party text of it survives here.  Four differences, each of
which the original article would have you look for:

    THERE IS NO powerfail LEVEL.  It wants a signal from an uninterruptable
    power supply, and this machine has no interface to one.

    THERE IS NO boot LEVEL.  It means "come back up automatically", which is
    the thing this hardware cannot do; `reboot' takes its place and halts.

    /etc/nologin IS NOT CREATED, and creating it would do nothing: this
    login(1) does not read it.  Logins remain possible during the countdown
    and are killed with everything else at the end.

    THERE IS NO "Some file systems remain mounted.  Proceed?" PROMPT.  A
    filesystem that is still mounted when the machine stops is as clean as one
    that was unmounted, provided the last thing to touch it was the sync -- so
    /etc/halt reports what it could not detach and syncs it anyway instead of
    asking whether to abandon a half-finished shutdown.

Interrupting shutdown during the countdown aborts it and the system remains
multi-user.  Once the final ten-second warning has been printed, the signals
are ignored and it goes.

